  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Art Van Elslander was an American furniture retailer who built Art Van Furniture from a single Detroit-area shop into a Midwestern chain. The son of a Belgian immigrant, he financed his first store through his home and insurance policies. He established a charitable foundation and sold the business in 2017.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 95,
      "band": "very_high",
      "reason": "Primary foundation history, congressional tribute and sale announcement corroborate the identity card."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2",
      "S3"
    ],
    "word_count": 50,
    "html": "<p>Art Van Elslander was an American furniture retailer who built Art Van Furniture from a single Detroit-area shop into a Midwestern chain. The son of a Belgian immigrant, he financed his first store through his home and insurance policies. He established a charitable foundation and sold the business in 2017.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Art Van Elslander spent years persuading Canadian retail executive Kim Yost to join his furniture business. When Yost arrived during a recession, Van Elslander gave him freedom to change almost everything, retaining the name on the stores. New product ranges and selling formats followed, while Van Elslander concentrated on expansion, property and advertising.\n\nHe also met weekly with six women from across the company to develop ways of reaching customers. One idea brought women into the store for an evening of refreshments and beauty treatments in their nightwear. Immediate selling was not the aim: the company tracked later purchases by the guests to judge the event.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 93,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Full trade interview provides recruitment and customer-event mechanisms without promotional claims."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S4"
    ],
    "word_count": 106,
    "html": "<p>Art Van Elslander spent years persuading Canadian retail executive Kim Yost to join his furniture business. When Yost arrived during a recession, Van Elslander gave him freedom to change almost everything, retaining the name on the stores. New product ranges and selling formats followed, while Van Elslander concentrated on expansion, property and advertising.</p>\r\n<p>He also met weekly with six women from across the company to develop ways of reaching customers. One idea brought women into the store for an evening of refreshments and beauty treatments in their nightwear. Immediate selling was not the aim: the company tracked later purchases by the guests to judge the event.</p>\r\n"
  },
